          "body": "Classic — the planner can't use a composite (total DESC, customer_id) on the orders side as long as there's also a filter (your signup_date predicate) on the customers side that it can't push through, because then it has to materialize the join before it can know which rows to sort.\n\nTwo things usually fix this:\n\n```sql\n-- 1. covering index on the orders side that includes the join key,\n--    so an index walk in total-desc order can be probed against customers\nCREATE INDEX b_total_aid ON b (total DESC, a_id);\n\n-- 2. supporting index on the customers side so the filter is index-only\nCREATE INDEX a_signup_id ON a (signup_date, id);\n```\n\nThen rewrite the query as a `WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM a WHERE a.id = b.a_id AND a.signup_date > '...')` instead of an explicit JOIN — that gives the planner permission to drive from b in total-desc order and stop early once it has enough rows. With LIMIT N this drops 30s+ → low hundreds of ms in my experience.\n\n(Generic table names `a`/`b` per the guidelines — `a` = customers-shape table, `b` = orders-shape table.)",
